    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C03"],
      "claim": "Cefazolin, which has a C-3 heterocyclic thiomethyl group but lacks the free alpha-amino side chain, shows only 1.5-2% oral bioavailability; this low result is less transferable because the query retains the free alpha-amino group important for intestinal absorption.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "The query is an alpha-aminocephalosporin with a p-hydroxyphenylglycine side chain, the same absorption-promoting motif found in amoxicillin, cefadroxil, and cephradine, all of which show high oral bioavailability. The closest analogue (C14) reports ~40% oral bioavailability despite a C-3 heterocyclic thiomethyl group. Low-F examples such as cefazolin and ceftriaxone lack the free alpha-amino side chain and are less transferable. The unfavorable physicochemical prior is overridden by this transferable experimental evidence, supporting F >= 20%.",
